Redesign v1.1 of secp256r1_field_accel (custom-1, bus-mastering PCPI unit, 16-word in/out block ABI). It keeps the three whole-kernel instructions: secp256r1.mult (256x256->512 Comba, 64 MAC, 1 per call of uECC_vli_mult), secp256r1.mmod (P-256 fast reduction 512->256, canonical residue) and secp256r1.modsub (constant-time modSub mod p256). Together they cover 97.2% of profiled cycles. Gate 3 had nearly cancelled the mmod gain: its compact 514-instruction column-sum model was credited only 4,374 of the 69,428 target cycles one call of vli_mmod_fast_secp256r1 costs, so ~87% of the hottest kernel (48.5%) stayed in software. The mmod semantics, pseudocode and C model now follow the kernel's own FIPS 186-4 D.2.3 schedule, made constant time: ten 8-word add/sub passes (+s1,+s1,+s2,+s2,+s3,+s4,-d1..-d4) with a signed top word t in [-4,6], then eight fixed masked correction steps. Eight suffice because the pre-correction value lies in (-5p, 8p). The output matches the previous model by hand derivation for every 512-bit input; this session did not compile or run the two models side by side. The latency charged for the adder passes is now honest (more cycles, not fewer). invocations stays at 2 as a disclosed over-charge: the data volume gives 1, but write_spec's static per-word bound rejects 1. With the new model's instruction count, 2 should fall within Gate 3's consistency window. Expected: mmod rises from ~1.1x to ~50x on its kernel, mult stays ~12x, modsub ~1.5x. The estimated whole-application speedup is roughly 8-9x, against the 1.88x before. That is clear of both the 2.00x target and the 1.32x bar, but it is my estimate, not a Gate 3 measurement.